WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:09.470
Film Courage: How has the release of the film
and its success impacted your life Ken?

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:16.170
Dr. Ken Atchity, Producer/Author: Well, once
answer would be not at all.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:21.660
But that would not really be a good answer
(a fun answer).

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:30.680
The real answer is it was disconcerting to
be validated for something that I believed

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:38.710
22 years ago and that I got a lot of other
people to believe 22 years ago.

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:43.630
Including Doubleday to the tune of 2 million
dollars and Disney to the tune of a million

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:47.409
and New Line to the tune of a million plus
and so on.

00:00:47.409 --> 00:00:52.781
And then it didn’t happen and then suddenly
all these years later it happened and people

00:00:52.781 --> 00:00:55.710
go “You must feel good to be corroborated?”

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:58.030
And I said “Yes I do.”

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:03.909
But the truth is it taught me the most important
lesson of all which is which I wrote into

00:01:03.909 --> 00:01:06.380
an essay called The Waiting Room [from Ken’s
blog].

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:11.890
If I had been waiting for THE MEG to happen
or for any movie that I started 20 years ago

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:17.030
to happen, I’d probably be miserable (if
not suicidal).

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:23.310
But what you do in the waiting room is you
do something else, that’s how you manager

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:24.310
your time.

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:32.210
When you’re waiting for something, that
can be annoying and a burden and what you

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:33.579
have to do is other things.

00:01:33.579 --> 00:01:40.020
So what I did was 50 other things and as a
result 30 movies have happened and hundreds

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:44.240
of books and a new publishing company and
lots of other things.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:51.162
Yes it’s satisfying to see that the world
endorses what Steve Alten [Author of Meg:

00:01:51.162 --> 00:01:57.909
A Novel of Deep Terror] and I believed in
22 years ago that this was a hugely popular

00:01:57.909 --> 00:02:00.040
subject for a story.

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:06.799
And all the way along brave people especially
Belle Avery who brought it home and Lorenzo

00:02:06.799 --> 00:02:13.220
di Bonaventura and other producers, they made
it happen too.

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:19.070
But I guess what it shows among other things
is don’t waste time hoping for something

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:20.070
to happen.

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:25.940
Do your work and then put it out in the world
and let the world take care of it, that’s

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:26.940
one thing.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:31.530
And then part of it is to trust the work that
happened.

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:38.230
When you create this baby (THE MEG in this
case), if it’s a good baby it will survive

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:41.750
and it will show its muscles when the time
comes.

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:46.030
Maybe it’s been in hiding for all these
years but suddenly it comes out and everybody

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:47.120
knows it.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:53.440
That’s great but what it tells the artist
I think is to focus on what’s at hand, what’s

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:59.570
in your workshop right now and do it well
and then don’t worry about things you can’t

00:02:59.570 --> 00:03:00.570
control.

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:03.400
Focus on what you can control.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:08.450
And I guess that’s my main feeling about
it is that we did a lot of work on THE MEG

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:14.460
at the beginning, we created its shape and
it finally came out and it did great and am

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:15.500
I surprised?

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:22.840
No, I’m pleased but not surprised because
I always believed it but I’m so glad I didn’t

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:28.450
hang my own personal psychology on it because
if I’d done that I’d have been locked

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:31.590
up by now.

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:37.970
Like myself, Steve went on to write eight
more books on different subjects too.

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:45.980
And built another career around his talent
and he’ll continue doing that.

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:49.370
He’s learned that lesson too that.

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:52.370
Was it disappointing that it didn’t come
out back then?

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:56.460
Well it felt like it at the time but in retrospect,
things are meant to be.

00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:03.430
And I always say to writers that I manage
that every project has its own clock and the

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:07.240
only problem is you can’t see the clock.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:15.080
So what you do is you put in the work the
best you can and then you screw the screws

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:22.030
on the cover and send it out into the world
and wish it well and turn to your next project

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:28.010
which hopefully you’ve done before you finish
this one project and that’s what the creator

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:29.010
does.

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:31.510
He keeps working on new projects.

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:34.030
So this world didn’t turn out perfectly.

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:37.380
God creates another world, maybe it is better?

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:40.030
Film Courage: How often do you create?

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:42.940
Ken: Do I create?

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:48.260
Well I am always involved in at least two
creative projects.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:55.600
I’ve just finished three screenplays in
the last 12 months and one book that was already

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:59.550
published and a book that’s about to be
published.

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:04.140
I never run out of creative things to do.

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:09.540
That’s what keeps me going is that creative
juice.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:14.530
That’s why I’m drawn to helping other
people with that creative juice because I

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:20.850
understand it having spent my lifetime living
it and kind of analyzing it.

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:26.560
Film Courage: If you could be remembered for
one quote, what would it be?

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:27.560
Your own quote.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:31.360
Ken: Oh my goodness, I don’t know?

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:38.240
I see my quotes on the Internet now and I
go “Well that’s good that I said that.”

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:46.830
I think that I would probably hope to be remembered
for Go For It!

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:48.730
And Never Give Up.

00:05:48.730 --> 00:05:50.020
But neither of those are original.

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:52.950
My mother is the one who said Go For It all
the time.

00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:56.080
And Never Give Up was Churchill.

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:08.020
I think those are the things that make a creative
writer sane is sticking to those two principles.

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:14.090
And don’t let anyone tell you that you can’t
do it or that you shouldn’t do it or even

00:06:14.090 --> 00:06:16.130
you shouldn’t do it this way.

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:23.180
Because if you have a clear vision of where
you are going you should stick to that vision

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:25.710
until you can’t anymore.

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:27.710
Film Courage: Or Just Do It!

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:29.240
But Nike has that one.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:30.410
Ken: Yes, Just Do It!

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:33.741
It’s funny how all these slogans are universal.

00:06:33.741 --> 00:06:36.190
They are not just about the creative world.

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:38.530
You know athletes are extremely creative.

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:43.500
I mean people who break records are breaking
them because of their creativity and because

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:49.790
they too have understood their mind and they
too have this kind of managing editor inside

00:06:49.790 --> 00:06:55.120
the mind that knows how to hold off the world
on one side and their vision of it on another

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:57.139
side.

00:06:57.139 --> 00:07:02.840
People say this particular high jump record
can’t be broken but they believe it can

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:06.760
and maybe they believe it because they had
a dream that they did it.

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:14.030
Or maybe they believe it because they calculated
that if this and if that then I could do it.

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:17.930
And maybe they just believe that of sheer
stubbornness.

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:22.990
But their only one way to test a belief in
the creative world and that is to do it.

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:25.420
To just do it.

00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:31.060
And every time it’s interesting, people
say this three-minute-mile couldn’t be run,

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:36.919
this two-minute-mile whatever but when somebody
breaks the record like that, within the next

00:07:36.919 --> 00:07:41.980
12 months it is matched or broken by three
other people.

00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:42.980
What does that tell you?

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:49.419
It tells you that the role of creativity in
human life is to keep us moving forward as

00:07:49.419 --> 00:07:50.630
a species.

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:57.110
It’s the creative people who have the vision
to say this could be done that hasn’t been

00:07:57.110 --> 00:08:03.360
done before, this could be done better, this
could be done differently and we listen to

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:07.010
that and we either belief it or we don’t
believe it.

00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:13.550
But at the end of the day if it works then
suddenly everybody is doing it and the continent

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:19.530
of reason is is reshaped by this eccentric
little island vision that came out of the

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:22.030
blue and suddenly people believe it.

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:28.199
I mean nobody would have know what to make
of Tweets or Instagram or Facebook just a

00:08:28.199 --> 00:08:29.270
few years ago.

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:37.909
2008 is when I think a lot of this really
began or earlier slightly (2006)?

00:08:37.909 --> 00:08:42.569
And now it’s hard to imagine the world without
it and that’s how quickly the continent

00:08:42.569 --> 00:08:47.600
gets changed by a creative change.

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:54.760
People looking at art after Picasso can no
longer see things the same way because he

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:58.170
came along and changed our way of seeing.

00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:01.829
And that’s the beauty of being involved
in the creative world.

